        <h2>Conclusion</h2>

        <p>
          The comparison reveals fundamental philosophical differences. Wikipedia's strength lies in its radical transparency—every decision, edit, and source is open to public scrutiny. This openness enables community self-correction and builds trust through verifiable processes.
        </p>

        <p>
          Grokipedia's AI-driven approach prioritizes speed and scale but sacrifices transparency and accountability. The absence of version history, unclear sourcing, and centralized control create opacity that undermines trust. Moreover, the platform's reliance on Wikipedia content—despite positioning itself as an alternative—suggests that algorithmic generation cannot yet replace human editorial judgment and community oversight.
        </p>

        <p>
          For users seeking reliable information, transparency remains the cornerstone of trust. Wikipedia's extensive documentation of its editorial processes provides accountability mechanisms that Grokipedia currently lacks. Until Grokipedia addresses these transparency deficits, it cannot offer the same level of reliability and verifiability that has made Wikipedia a trusted reference for over two decades.
        </p>

